	regionKind = "zone",
	kind = "region",
	sections = {
		{
			title = "Summary",
			text = [=[The Searing Gorge is a wound in the southern mountains of Khaz Modan. Its cliffs are black, its soil is ash, and its air shimmers with heat that rises from the stone itself. At its heart the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} have dug an immense pit they call {link:the-cauldron}the Cauldron{/link}, and around it their war golems stamp and hiss, their slaves labor without rest, and creatures of living fire walk the rocks as if the land were theirs. To the south, the smoking bulk of {link:blackrock-mountain}Blackrock Mountain{/link} fills the sky. To the north-east, a locked gate stands between the gorge and the green valleys of {link:loch-modan}Loch Modan{/link}. Few come here by choice, and fewer still stay. Those who do are hard folk: renegade dwarves, lonely artisans, treasure hunters and schemers of every kind.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Origins",
			text = [=[The gorge was not always a gorge. Before the fire, these were mountain valleys in the lands of the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Iron clan{/link}, not far from their capital, the city of {link:ruins-of-thaurissan}Thaurissan{/link}. When the High King of the dwarves died, the Bronzebeards, the Wildhammers and the Dark Irons fought for his crown in the {red:war-of-the-three-hammers}War of the Three Hammers{/red}. The Dark Irons lost. Their sorcerer-thane Thaurissan, desperate for a power that could turn the war, reached into the depths of the world and called up {red:ragnaros}Ragnaros{/red} the Firelord. The coming of that elemental lord tore the land apart. The city was lost, {link:blackrock-mountain}Blackrock Mountain{/link} rose where it had stood, the country to the south was burned into the {link:burning-steppes}Burning Steppes{/link}, and the valleys to the north split open into the chasm that the dwarves of {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link} now call the Searing Gorge.

The Dark Irons who survived became the Firelord's servants. The dwarven historians of Ironforge record that, when they had begun again inside the mountain, they fortified {link:the-cauldron}the Cauldron{/link} of the gorge and set to digging. What they seek there, no one outside their halls can say.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "The Present Day",
			text = [=[The gorge belongs to the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link}. Their camp sprawls over {link:firewatch-ridge}Firewatch Ridge{/link}, their sentries hold four stone towers about the rim of {link:the-cauldron}the Cauldron{/link}, and their overseers drive gangs of captives through the tunnels of {link:the-slag-pit}the Slag Pit{/link}. The dig site at {link:grimesilt-dig-site}Grimesilt{/link}, once worked by the dwarves of {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link}, is in their hands.

They are not the only power here. In the north-west, cultists of the {link:twilight-s-hammer}Twilight's Hammer{/link} have made a camp among the high rocks and worship there in the name of the {red:old-gods}Old Gods{/red}. On a mesa above the Cauldron, a band of Dark Iron smiths who turned their backs on the mountain keeps a fortified town called {link:thorium-point}Thorium Point{/link}. These are the dwarves of the {link:thorium-brotherhood}Thorium Brotherhood{/link}, and they pay well for help against their former kin. Gryphons and wind riders both land at their post, for the Brotherhood takes coin from {link:alliance}Alliance{/link} and {link:horde}Horde{/link} alike.

The road in is hard. From {link:loch-modan}Loch Modan{/link}, the way through the {link:dustfire-valley}Stonewrought Pass{/link} is barred by a gate whose key is kept by {link:mountaineer-pebblebitty}Mountaineer Pebblebitty{/link}, and she does not give it lightly. From the {link:badlands}Badlands{/link}, a steep path climbs into the gorge from the lands near {link:kargath}Kargath{/link}. From the south, the only road passes beneath the ash-choked flanks of {link:blackrock-mountain}Blackrock{/link} itself.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Inhabitants",
			text = [=[• {b}The Dark Iron dwarves.{/b} Geologists, slavers, taskmasters, steamsmiths and marksmen. They rule the gorge in the Firelord's name and guard it with golems of their own making.
• {b}The {link:thorium-brotherhood}Thorium Brotherhood{/link}.{/b} {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} who left the mountain of their own will. They mine, smith and keep watch from {link:thorium-point}Thorium Point{/link}.
• {b}The slaves of {link:the-cauldron}the Cauldron{/link}.{/b} Captives of many peoples, driven so hard for so long that some of them turn on anyone who interferes with their work, even those who come to free them.
• {b}The {link:twilight-s-hammer}Twilight's Hammer{/link}.{/b} Idolaters, geomancers, dark shamans and fire guards who hold the north-western heights of {link:firewatch-ridge}Firewatch Ridge{/link}.
• {b}Creatures of fire.{/b} Blazing, inferno and magma elementals walk the gorge. In the lava pools of {link:the-slag-pit}the Slag Pit{/link} dwell the lizards called incendosaurs.
• {b}Beasts of the burned land.{/b} Great lava spiders, glassweb spiders, fire beetles and lava crabs. In the south-east roams {link:margol-the-rager}Margol the Rager{/link}, a thunder lizard of enormous size.
• {b}Strangers.{/b} A lone leatherworker, a woman with an infernal at her side, a man who seeks vengeance, traders of the shadowy Undermarket, and, it is said, a {link:dwarves}dwarf{/link} who has been locked in an outhouse.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "History",
			text = [=[• {b}The Green Valleys.{/b} No chronicle records what the valleys were called before the fire, nor who lived in them beyond the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Iron clan{/link}.
• {b}The War of the Three Hammers.{/b} Some two hundred and thirty years before the {link:dark-portal}Dark Portal{/link} opened, the Dark Irons made war on {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link} and {link:grim-batol}Grim Batol{/link}, and were broken.
• {b}The Summoning.{/b} {link:ruins-of-thaurissan}Thaurissan{/link}'s call brought forth Ragnaros. The mountain rose, the steppes burned, and the gorge was torn open.
• {b}{link:the-cauldron}The Cauldron{/link}.{/b} The Dark Irons, now bound to the Firelord, fortified the gorge and began the great excavation at its center.
• {b}The Present Day.{/b} Ironforge has sent archaeologists into the gorge, and some have not come back. The {link:thorium-brotherhood}Thorium Brotherhood{/link} has raised its town on the northern cliffs. The {link:twilight-s-hammer}Twilight's Hammer{/link} has come to worship among the fires.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Lore of the Gorge",
			text = [=[• The {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} say their four sentry towers about {link:the-cauldron}the Cauldron{/link} can never be destroyed. It is also said that they can be made to burn with a fire no element can put out.
• {link:margol-the-rager}Margol the Rager{/link} was the bane of every archaeologist who ever set foot in the gorge. A dwarven mountaineer's axe head was once found broken off in her horn.
• High on {link:firewatch-ridge}Firewatch Ridge{/link}, the {link:twilight-s-hammer}Twilight's Hammer{/link} keep a cage. Inside it is the spirit of one of their own, left there as a warning to anyone who would betray the cult.
• The captain of {link:thorium-brotherhood}the Thorium Brotherhood{/link}'s lookouts is blind. He claims that ale has sharpened his remaining senses beyond those of any sighted {link:dwarves}dwarf{/link}.
• Somewhere near the old dig at {link:grimesilt-dig-site}Grimesilt{/link} stands a wooden outhouse whose key, the Dark Irons have written, is not to be copied.]=],
		},
	},
})

